        "text": "It is natural for man to come to understanding through things sensed, because all our knowledge begins in the senses.",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.1, a.9; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1001.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1001.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "We call beautiful those things that please when seen.",
        "topics": [
          "joy"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.5, a.4, ad 1; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1003.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1005.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Since to love is nothing other than to will good to someone, God loves all things that exist.",
        "topics": [
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.20, a.2; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1020.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "God’s love pours out and creates goodness in things.",
        "topics": [
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.20, a.2; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1015.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1020.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Among all the virtues that concern our neighbor, mercy is the greatest.",
        "topics": [
          "mercy",
          "charity"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.30, a.4; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth3027.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3030.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Charity is a kind of friendship of man with God.",
        "topics": [
          "charity",
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.23, a.1; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth3023.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3023.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "It is ridiculous to say that one has friendship for wine or for a horse.",
        "topics": [
          "love",
          "joy"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.23, a.1; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth3023.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3023.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Law is an ordinance of reason for the common good, made by the one who has care of the community, and promulgated.",
        "topics": [
          "justice"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I-II, q.90, a.4; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth2090.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/2090.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Good is to be done and pursued, and evil is to be avoided.",
        "topics": [
          "justice",
          "sin"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I-II, q.94, a.2; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth2094.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/2094.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "As it is greater to illuminate than only to shine, so it is greater to hand on to others what one has contemplated than only to contemplate.",
        "topics": [
          "prayer",
          "charity"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.188, a.6; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth3183.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3188.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "The mind of man would break if it were never relaxed from its tension.",
        "topics": [
          "peace",
          "joy"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.168, a.2; Leonine Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth3155.html; Dominican Fathers 1920 English: https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3168.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Of all human pursuits, the pursuit of wisdom is the more perfect, the more sublime, the more useful, and the more agreeable.",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom",
          "joy"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa contra Gentiles I.2, tr. Joseph Rickaby, Of God and His Creatures (1905). https://archive.org/details/OfGodAndHisCreatures"
      },
      {
        "text": "Truth must be the last end of the whole universe.",
        "topics": [
          "truth"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, Summa contra Gentiles I.1; Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/scg1001.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "A small error at the start is a great one at the end.",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom",
          "truth"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, De ente et essentia, prologue (citing Aristotle, De caelo I); Latin, our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/oee.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Human sight can be deceived, but God’s knowledge never fails.",
        "topics": [
          "faith"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, sermons on the Creed (In Symbolum Apostolorum), prologue, as taken down by Reginald of Piperno; our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/csv.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Three things are necessary for man’s salvation: knowledge of what to believe, of what to desire, and of what to do.",
        "topics": [
          "faith",
          "devotion"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas Aquinas, sermons on the commandments (De decem praeceptis), prologue, as taken down by Peter of Andria; our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/cac.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Everything I have written seems to me like straw compared with what I have seen and what has been revealed to me.",
        "topics": [
          "humility",
          "prayer"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas to Reginald, Dec. 1273, as Bartholomew of Capua swore at Naples, 1319 (art. 79; hearsay from Reginald); Fontes vitae S. Thomae fasc. 4, p. 377; our translation. https://archive.org/details/fontesvitaesthom00pr"
      },
      {
        "text": "In truth I would rather have Chrysostom on Matthew.",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom",
          "humility"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas at Paris, as Bartholomew of Capua reported at Naples, 1319 (art. 78); Fontes vitae S. Thomae fasc. 4, p. 376; our translation. https://archive.org/details/fontesvitaesthom00pr"
      },
      {
        "text": "Reginald, in no state can I be as useful to our Order as in this one.",
        "topics": [
          "humility"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas to Reginald of Piperno, 1274, as Bartholomew of Capua reported at Naples, 1319 (art. 78); Fontes vitae S. Thomae fasc. 4, p. 375; our translation. https://archive.org/details/fontesvitaesthom00pr"
      },
      {
        "text": "I receive you, provision for my pilgrimage, for love of whom I have studied, kept watch, and worked.",
        "topics": [
          "devotion",
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "Thomas at his last Communion, Fossanova, March 1274, as William of Tocco gives it (Ystoria, ch. 58); Fontes vitae S. Thomae fasc. 1, p. 132; our translation. https://archive.org/details/prummerfontesvitaestho"
      },
      {
        "text": "Dare as much as you can, for he is greater than all praise, and you are not able to praise enough.",
        "topics": [
          "devotion",
          "faith"
        ],
        "source": "Lauda Sion, sequence for Corpus Christi, ascribed to Thomas Aquinas (authorship probable); our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/pcx.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Jesus, whom now I see veiled, when will that come about for which I thirst so much?",
        "topics": [
          "devotion",
          "hope"
        ],
        "source": "Adoro te devote, ascribed to Thomas Aquinas (authorship probable; Wielockx ed., 2007); our translation. https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/pat.html"
      }
    ],
    "prayers": [
      {
        "title": "Adoro te devote (lines)",
        "text": "Sight, touch, and taste are deceived in you, but by hearing alone it is safe to believe. … I believe whatever the Son of God has said; nothing is truer than this word of truth. … I do not see the wounds, as Thomas did, yet I confess you my God. … Loving pelican, Lord Jesus, cleanse me, unclean, with your blood. … Jesus, whom now I see veiled, when will that come about for which I thirst so much?",
        "author": "Thomas Aquinas (ascribed; authorship probable)",
        "source": "Corpus Thomisticum, ed. R. Wielockx (2007), https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/pat.html",
        "rights": "Latin 13th century, public domain; the 2007 edition is copyrighted, so quote only a line or two of Latin; English is our translation",
        "note": "Partial: only the lines checked in the notes."
      },
      {
        "title": "Pange lingua, with Tantum ergo (lines)",
        "text": "The Word made flesh by a word makes true bread his flesh … and if the senses fail, faith alone suffices to make a sincere heart firm. … Bowing low, let us venerate so great a sacrament … let faith supply what the senses lack.",
        "author": "Thomas Aquinas (ascribed; Ptolemy of Lucca c. 1317; authorship probable)",
        "source": "Officium “Sacerdos,” Corpus Thomisticum (text of C. Lambot / P.-M. Gy), https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/pcx.html",
        "rights": "Latin public domain; edition copyrighted; English is our translation",
        "note": "Partial; the full hymn needs a fresh translation from a public-domain Latin text."
      },
      {
        "title": "Lauda Sion (lines)",
        "text": "Dare as much as you can, for he is greater than all praise, and you are not able to praise enough. … What you do not grasp, what you do not see, a lively faith confirms, beyond the order of things.",
        "author": "Thomas Aquinas (ascribed; authorship probable)",
        "source": "Officium “Sacerdos,” Corpus Thomisticum, https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/pcx.html",
        "rights": "Latin public domain; edition copyrighted; English is our translation",
        "note": "Partial."
      },
      {
        "title": "Sacris solemniis (line)",
        "text": "Let old things pass away, let all be new: hearts, voices, and works.",
        "author": "Thomas Aquinas (ascribed; authorship probable)",
        "source": "Officium “Sacerdos,” Corpus Thomisticum, https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/pcx.html",
        "rights": "Latin public domain; edition copyrighted; English is our translation",
        "note": "Partial. The “Panis angelicus” stanza of this hymn was not located in the text read; do not quote it until checked."
      }
    ],
    "prayers_note": "Not saved as prayers: “Creator ineffabilis” (prayer before study) is traditionally attributed to Thomas (Wikipedia notes Pius XI’s Studiorum Ducem, 1923, not read); no proof either way was found. “Concede mihi, misericors Deus” is attributed; a secondary page says Tocco includes it, but the words were not found in Tocco’s Latin. Neither text was collected from a primary source. Mark both “attributed” if added later.",
    "books": [],
    "books_note": "None saved. The Summa Theologiae in the Dominican Fathers translation (1920, New Advent) and the Summa contra Gentiles in Rickaby’s translation (1905, Internet Archive) are public-domain texts linked in the sources; no edition record was prepared."
  },
  "share_kit": {
    "tagline": "After one Mass in 1273 he never wrote again. He said his work was straw.",
    "did_you_know": [
      {
        "fact": "His own brothers seized Thomas on the road and held him captive in the family castles to keep him from becoming a Dominican friar.",
        "card": "notes-witnesses §3 (Tocco pp. 72–74; Bartholomew of Capua, process p. 372)"
      },
      {
        "fact": "When a friar admired Paris and said Thomas could sell it to the king to build Dominican houses, Thomas said he would rather have Chrysostom on Matthew.",
        "card": "notes-witnesses §5 Q8; notes-own-words, Process art. 78 (p. 376)"
      },
      {
        "fact": "At King Louis IX’s table Thomas struck the table and announced that the argument against the Manichees was settled, and the king sent for a scribe.",
        "card": "notes-witnesses §5 Q9, scene 6 (Tocco pp. 116–117)"
      },
      {
        "fact": "Two months after his death the Paris Faculty of Arts asked the Dominicans for his bones “as the greatest gift.”",
        "card": "notes-witnesses §3 and §5 Extra (Documenta pp. 583–586)"
      },
      {
        "fact": "In the Summa he wrote that the human mind would break if it were never relaxed, and he named a virtue for play: eutrapelia.",
        "card": "notes-own-words Q16 (ST II-II q.168 a.2)"
      }
    ],
    "quote_cards": [
      {
        "text": "The least that can be known of the highest things is more to be desired than the most certain knowledge of the smallest things.",
        "attribution": "St. Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q. 1, a. 5, ad 1 (our translation)"
      },
      {
        "text": "Since grace does not destroy nature but perfects it, natural reason must serve faith.",
        "attribution": "St. Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q. 1, a. 8, ad 2 (our translation)"
      },
      {
        "text": "Charity is a kind of friendship of man with God.",
        "attribution": "St. Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q. 23, a. 1 (our translation)"
      }
    ],
    "share_moments": [
      {
        "moment": "“Reginald, I cannot”: the morning Thomas stopped writing and called his work straw",
        "section_id": "reginald-i-cannot"
      },
      {
        "moment": "Dinner with St. Louis: “Now it is settled against the Manichaean heresy!”",
        "section_id": "at-the-table-of-the-king"
      },
      {
        "moment": "Fossanova: “for love of whom I have studied, kept watch, and worked”",
        "section_id": "this-is-my-rest-forever"
      }
    ],
    "for_someone_who": [
      "For anyone who loves to study and wonders what it is all for",
      "For anyone whose family fought their vocation",
      "For anyone who has given years to work that now seems small"
    ],
    "read_next": [
      {
        "slug": "albert-the-great",
        "sentence": "Albert taught Thomas, and in a legend first written about seventy years later he said the “dumb ox” would bellow through the whole world.",
        "on_site": false
      },
      {
        "slug": "bonaventure",
