  "gaps": [
    "Biography: 8,018 words by the lint count (narrative plus the source note and research paragraph), at the low end of tier A; 14 story chapters, 10 block quotes; lint has no MUST FIX lines and 3 CHECK lines for long sentences inside quotations.",
    "Not read: the Fossanova testimonies of 1321, Gui’s death chapters, the full Naples process, Pius XI’s Studiorum Ducem, Torrell, Weisheipl, the Sentences commentary, the Quodlibets, the Latin of the bull, Mirabilis Deus (1567), the Acta Sanctorum, Gerard de Frachet’s own text.",
    "Straw saying: only Bartholomew of Capua’s sworn hearsay (from Reginald). An earlier note says Bartholomew heard it about Sept. 1303 through John del Giudice of Anagni; not verified. Tocco, Calo and Gui give the San Severino version without “straw.”",
    "Dumb ox and talking crucifix: first in Calo, Tocco and Gui (c. 1317–1323); not in the 1319 depositions; told as legend.",
    "Conflicts attributed in the text: jailer (Tocco and Gui: mother; Bartholomew: father); escape (Tocco: mother let him down; Ptolemy: Thomas arranged it); herrings (Peter: ate; Tocco and Abbot Nicholas: refused); funeral preacher (Tocco’s life: Reginald; Tocco’s testimony: Peter of Sezze); days from last Communion to death (Tocco: unction next day; Peter: third day).",
    "Unverified: Landulf’s death year (would bear on the jailer question); birth year (c. 1225 from Tocco, 1227 in Calo and Baring-Gould); age at entry (Gui 14, Ptolemy 16); the exact Panis angelicus stanza; the 1303 chain; the Mirabilis Deus day (11 or 15 April 1567); the 1969 decree date.",
    "Wrongly credited or unproven lines kept out of the quotes: “Hominem unius libri timeo” (first credited by Jeremy Taylor, 1649); “To one who has faith, no explanation is necessary” (Werfel film, 1943); “The things that we love tell us what we are” (no locator); De modo studendi (Ignotus Auctor per Corpus Thomisticum); Creator ineffabilis and Concede mihi (attributed only); “as many miracles as articles” (tradition).",
    "Reported words: the Credo and Ten Commandments sermons are reportationes (Reginald; Peter of Andria) and are marked “as taken down.” The Fossanova viaticum prayer is Tocco’s version; Peter’s sworn version is shorter.",
    "Rights: Corpus Thomisticum digital editions are copyrighted; only a line or two of Latin is quoted, with our own English. Dominican Fathers 1920 and Rickaby 1905 are public domain. The FSSPX bull translation is facts-only; only the short phrase on life and miracles is paraphrased.",
    "Several Tocco and Gui catalogue miracles are given in one sentence each because the notes hold only the outline; the tooth and the sickroom star likewise.",
